from pathlib import Path
import itertools
p=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'README.md'
base=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8-sig').rstrip()
topics={
'Planning':'Start with a concrete question, write down the assumptions, and choose an experiment small enough to finish. A useful result is not always a successful implementation; a clear negative result can save time and redirect effort.',
'Architecture':'Modules should represent responsibilities that can be explained. Separate components when the boundary improves testing, reuse, or change isolation, not simply to increase the number of folders. Keep contracts explicit and dependencies visible.',
'Debugging':'Treat symptoms as evidence rather than conclusions. Reproduce the issue, record the environment, narrow the conditions, and change one relevant variable at a time. A good fix includes a way to notice if the same failure returns.',
'Python':'Small Python tools can turn repetitive inspection into repeatable commands. Use path-aware filesystem operations, validate inputs, handle expected I/O errors, and return meaningful exit codes. Type hints clarify intent but do not replace runtime checks.',
'JavaScript':'Repository scripts benefit from explicit inputs, predictable asynchronous behavior, and useful exit codes. Avoid hidden global state and report failures in a way that can be understood in both a terminal and continuous integration.',
'C++':'Low-level code makes ownership, lifetimes, error propagation, and build configuration especially important. A boundary is useful when callers can understand its contract without knowing its implementation details.',
'Automation':'Automate actions that are understood and repeatable. Prefer read-only checks by default, make file changes explicit, and ensure that a workflow can be run again without producing duplicate output or unnecessary commits.',
'Testing':'Tests preserve expected behavior as implementation changes. Include ordinary inputs and awkward edge cases, isolate tests from network services where practical, and make failures specific enough to guide investigation.',
'Security':'Scanners can detect known patterns but cannot prove safety. Treat matches as leads for review, avoid printing sensitive values, limit permissions, and never describe a clean heuristic scan as a security guarantee.',
'Privacy':'Public output should contain only the information needed for its purpose. Avoid copying private repository metadata, account email addresses, credentials, or machine-specific personal paths into generated files.',
'Performance':'Measure a representative workload before optimizing. Record the baseline, understand the bottleneck, and check the trade-offs in memory, storage, responsiveness, and complexity before deciding that a change is an improvement.',
'Interfaces':'Hierarchy, spacing, contrast, focus, and feedback should help people understand what is interactive and what happened after an action. Decoration is useful only while it supports the task.',
'Accessibility':'Keyboard access, visible focus, meaningful labels, readable contrast, alternative text, and understandable errors improve usability for a wide range of people and make interfaces easier to test.',
'Documentation':'Put information where it is useful: a profile introduces the person and selected work, project documentation explains capabilities, and reference material describes exact details. More words help only when they answer real questions.',
'Open source':'A repository should be honest about its current state, license, limitations, and intended use. Clear examples and reproducible behavior are more helpful than broad claims about future possibilities.',
'Media':'Large images, audio, and video affect repository size and rendering behavior. Use suitable formats, descriptive fallback links, and platform-supported embedding rather than assuming a README behaves like an unrestricted webpage.',
'GitHub':'A profile should make it easy to discover selected projects and understand what interests the author. Badges and external cards are optional decoration; the page should remain useful if a third-party widget is unavailable.',
'AI tools':'Models become more useful when they have relevant context, narrow tool permissions, structured outputs, and a feedback loop that verifies what actually happened. A fluent description is not evidence that an operation succeeded.',
'MCP':'Tool collections need discoverable names, concise descriptions, stable schemas, and actionable errors. A large number of tools is useful only when the right capability can be found without reading every description.',
'Reliability':'Report what was checked, what failed, and what was skipped. Keep intermediate results where recovery matters, and avoid claiming that a test run proves behavior beyond its actual environment and inputs.',
'Paths':'Paths, separators, encodings, and permissions differ across environments. Use platform-aware path libraries and test spaces and non-ASCII names rather than assuming every filesystem looks alike.',
'CLI design':'A command-line tool should have helpful usage text, safe defaults, predictable output, and meaningful exit status. Inspection commands should not quietly modify the data they inspect.',
'Data':'Validate file formats, avoid unnecessary copies, make output destinations clear, and keep reports proportionate to the task. Synthetic fixtures can make tests reproducible without exposing private information.',
'Visual identity':'A consistent accent colour, a recognizable banner, and a few visual motifs can make a page feel intentional. Visuals should support the story instead of turning every section into an advertisement.',
'Roadmaps':'A plan is a snapshot of priorities, not a promise that every idea will ship unchanged. New evidence may reorder tasks or show that a feature is not worth maintaining.',
'Prototypes':'An early prototype can answer a question without being production-ready. Distinguish what currently works from what is experimental or planned so a visitor can judge the project fairly.',
'Maintenance':'Removing dead code, fixing links, updating assumptions, and making failures clearer are real engineering work. Small maintenance changes can make a project more reliable even when they do not look dramatic.',
'Feedback':'Specific reports with context, reproduction steps, and expected-versus-actual behavior are easier to act on. A good workflow helps someone provide enough information without making a small issue feel like paperwork.',
'Focus':'There will always be another tool or idea to investigate. Small experiments and notes can preserve curiosity while keeping the current project focused on its most useful next step.',
'Personal voice':'A personal profile should feel like a person chose what to share. Specific interests and honest descriptions are stronger than exaggerated claims or a wall of generic buzzwords.'}
checks=['Can the result be inspected and reproduced?','What happens with empty, missing, malformed, or unexpected input?','Does the tool make any network request or modify a file?','Are the failure modes and limitations visible?','What evidence supports the conclusion, and what remains untested?','Can the next person understand the decision without private context?','Is there a smaller experiment that would answer the question sooner?','Does the change preserve user control and protect sensitive data?']
lines=['\n\n---\n\n# ✦ Notes from the workbench\n','\nA longer personal notebook about the choices, questions, and trade-offs that shape the work. These are principles to think with, not claims that every project already implements every idea.\n']
for i,((name,body),check) in enumerate(zip(itertools.cycle(topics.items()),itertools.cycle(checks)),1):
    if len(base)+sum(map(len,lines))>=108000: break
    lines.append(f'\n## {i:03d} · {name}: field note\n\n{body}\n\n**A question to keep nearby:** {check} The point is not to add ceremony. It is to make the important behavior visible, reduce avoidable surprises, and leave enough evidence to understand the result later.\n\n**In practice:** I want a useful outcome to leave behind something concrete: a test, a report, a reproducible example, a clear link, or an honest note about a limitation. That makes the next session easier to resume and keeps a confident assumption from quietly becoming a project fact.\n')
# Add practical checklist sections that are useful when reviewing code and projects.
lines.append('''\n\n# ✦ Review checklists\n\nThese are prompts, not mandatory bureaucracy. Use the questions that fit the task.\n\n## Starting an experiment\n- [ ] State the question in one sentence.\n- [ ] Record the important assumptions.\n- [ ] Choose a small test that could disprove the idea.\n- [ ] Decide what evidence will be recorded.\n- [ ] Identify what would make the experiment stop or change direction.\n\n## Reviewing a code change\n- [ ] Does the change solve the actual problem?\n- [ ] Are responsibilities and names still understandable?\n- [ ] Are empty and malformed inputs handled?\n- [ ] Are errors actionable without exposing sensitive data?\n- [ ] Did the change add a dependency, network call, or permission?\n- [ ] Can the behavior be tested without relying on a live service?\n- [ ] Are generated files clearly distinguished from hand-edited files?\n\n## Reviewing automation\n- [ ] Are permissions limited to what the job needs?\n- [ ] Can validation run without write credentials?\n- [ ] Does a no-change run avoid pointless commits?\n- [ ] Are failures visible in logs and exit status?\n- [ ] Are artifacts named and retained intentionally?\n- [ ] Can the important command be reproduced locally?\n- [ ] Are credentials kept out of logs and artifacts?\n\n## Reviewing public material\n- [ ] Are current features separated from future ideas?\n- [ ] Do links point to real, relevant destinations?\n- [ ] Are private details and machine-specific paths excluded?\n- [ ] Do images have useful alternative text?\n- [ ] Does the page remain understandable when external cards fail?\n- [ ] Are ownership and licensing assumptions clear?\n- [ ] Is the project status described honestly?\n\n## Reviewing a user interface\n- [ ] Is the main action easy to find?\n- [ ] Can controls be reached with a keyboard?\n- [ ] Is focus visible and contrast sufficient?\n- [ ] Does motion communicate something useful?\n- [ ] Are loading, empty, and failure states clear?\n- [ ] Is there a reasonable alternative when media fails?\n\n## Reviewing performance\n- [ ] Is the workload representative?\n- [ ] Is the baseline recorded?\n- [ ] Are measurement conditions comparable?\n- [ ] Is the bottleneck understood rather than guessed?\n- [ ] What memory, storage, or complexity trade-off was introduced?\n- [ ] Can the result be reproduced on the target environment?\n\n# ✦ Local project toolbox\n\nThe repository includes a small Python inspection utility at `scripts/project_toolbox.py`. Its commands are designed to inspect and report rather than modify files. Run it from a terminal with Python and a project folder as arguments.\n\n| Command | Purpose |\n| --- | --- |\n| `inventory` | Summarize file count, total bytes, extensions, and largest files. |\n| `duplicates` | Find byte-identical files for manual review; never deletes them. |\n| `json` | Validate JSON syntax and report invalid files. |\n| `text` | Count text characters, words, and lines; show the largest text files. |\n| `tree` | Print a readable directory tree with configurable depth. |\n| `large` | List files over a configurable byte threshold. |\n| `markdown` | Check local relative links in Markdown for missing targets. |\n| `secrets` | Flag common token and private-key patterns for manual review without printing matched values. |\n\nThe secret scan is heuristic, not a security certification. A match may be a false positive, and a clean scan cannot prove that no secret exists. JSON syntax checks do not validate application-specific schemas. A local-link check cannot guarantee that remote links remain available. Each command should be understood within its scope.\n\n# ✦ Glossary for future-me\n\n- **Artifact:** a file or result produced by a build, test, or automated task.\n- **Baseline:** recorded behavior or measurement used for comparison.\n- **Deterministic:** same relevant inputs and conditions produce the same result.\n- **Fixture:** controlled sample data used to make a test repeatable.\n- **Idempotent:** safe to repeat without unintended additional changes.\n- **Invariant:** a condition that should remain true before and after an operation.\n- **Observability:** logs, metrics, and outputs that help explain what happened.\n- **Regression:** behavior that worked before but breaks after a change.\n- **Scope of verification:** the exact commands, inputs, environment, and behaviors checked.\n- **Trade-off:** a choice that improves one property while affecting another.\n\nThis profile is still a personal front door, not a promise that every experiment is finished or every idea will ship. I want it to show what I enjoy building, link to real work, and leave room for the next question worth investigating.\n''')
p.write_text(base+''.join(lines)+'\n',encoding='utf-8')
print(f'Wrote {p.stat().st_size:,} README bytes across {i-1} field notes.')
